            <ArticleTitle>The evolution of minimally invasive cardiac surgery: from minimal access to transcatheter approaches.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>75-87</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/fca-2017-0048</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The field of minimally invasive cardiac surgery has undergone rapid transformation over recent years. In this review, we provide a summary of the most current evidence supporting the use of minimally invasive aortic and mitral valve replacement techniques, as well as transcatheter approaches for aortic and mitral valve disease. As an adjunct, the use of robotically assisted coronary bypass surgery and hybrid coronary revascularization procedures is discussed. In order to obtain optimal patient outcomes, a collaborative, heart-team approach between cardiac surgeons and interventional cardiologists is necessary.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
